          %h2#idea Idea
          %p Game theory is the study of strategic interaction between agents. It is often used for mathematical modelling of real-world situations in which agents interact, including in microeconomics, computer science and ecology.
          %p
            Game theory should not be confused with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/game+semantics"} game semantics
          %h2#via_coalgebras Via coalgebras
          %p
            Several authors have approached game theory through coalgebraic systems theory, defining games as elements of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra+for+an+endofunctor"} terminal coalgebra
            of an appropriate functor. This puts the concepts of
            %em state
            and
            %em state transition
            in a game to centre stage. It is closely related to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extensive-form_game"} extensive form games
          %p
            Perhaps the first paper to use this approach is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Pavlovic09"} Pavlovic 2009
            %a{:href => "#AbramskyWinschel17"} Abramsky and Winschel 2017
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Ghani18a"} Ghani, Kupke, Lambert and Forsberg 2018
          %h2#compositional_game_theory Compositional game theory
          %p
            Open games approach games as open systems, making them morphisms of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            which can be depicted as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagrams
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/applied+category+theory"} applied category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Ghani18b"} Ghani, Hedges, Winschel and Zahn 2018
          %p
            Open games have a close connection to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lens+%28in+computer+science%29"} lenses
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            in a game-theoretically interesting way, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Hedges18"} Hedges 2018
          %h2#topology_and_game_theory Topology and game theory
          %p
            Deformations between strategy profiles have been considered as a practical method to compute Nash equilibria (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Herings10"} Herings and Peeters 2010
